FILED s543q (Opus 5.5, 2026-11-28, CODE 496df8b1 = MAIN-READY-113 caf9c693, v2-16400) by the Rosetta census B (docs/rosetta-plan.md step 1, section 6b; #2104).

THE SHAPE (neutral reproducer, run on the tree, perl 5.40.3 vs PCL):

    package Tally;
    use overload
        '+=' => sub { my ($t, $n) = @_; $t->{log} .= "+$n"; $t },
        '-=' => sub { my ($t, $n) = @_; $t->{log} .= "-$n"; $t },
        '.=' => sub { my ($t, $s) = @_; $t->{log} .= ".$s"; $t },
        '""' => sub { "Tally[" . $_[0]{log} . "]" };
    sub new { bless { log => "" }, shift }
    package main;
    my $t = Tally->new;
    $t += 3;
    $t -= 1;
    $t .= "x";
    print ref($t) || "plain", " $t\n";

    perl: Tally Tally[+3-1.x]
    PCL : plain 2x          (exit 0, nothing on stderr)

    With BOTH `+=` and `+` defined (`'+' => sub { "plus" }`): perl calls `+=` (the object stays a Tally), PCL calls `+` and $t becomes the string "plus".

WHERE (emitted Lisp): the handlers ARE registered -- `(p-register-overloads "Tally" (... "+=" ... "-=" ... ".=" ...))` -- but the statements compile to `(p-incf $t 3)`, `(p-decf $t 1)`, `(p-.= $t "x")`, and the compound family never asks for the assignment key: #900 (done) taught it to AUTOGENERATE `+=` from a `+` handler, the explicit `+=` key is not looked up before that.  perl's rule (perldoc overload, "Assignments" and "Minimal Set"): `$a += $b` calls the `+=` handler when there is one and assigns its result to $a; only without it does it fall back to `+`.

CHEAP DISCRIMINATING MEASUREMENT (taken): 1253-Set with its two `+=` sites spelled as method calls (`$x->add($_)`, `$s->add(...)`) is byte-identical to perl -- this is the program's only cause; its LOUD face (a die "Can't use string (\"18\") as a HASH ref") is the number the object became.

PROGRAMS: 1253-Set.
